<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary adrenal insufficiency (autoimmune Addison&apos;s disease) (E27.1)</content>. The 21-hydroxylase antibodies from intake are positive (18 U/mL), confirming an autoimmune cause; with the hospital&apos;s low morning cortisol and high ACTH before treatment, no further diagnostic testing is needed. Hydrocortisone 30 mg a day is more than replacement needs. Mineralocorticoid replacement is too low: salt craving, a postural drop of 14 mmHg, sodium 134 and potassium 5.2 mmol/L, and plasma renin activity 9.6 ng/mL/h at intake. Dr. Desai agrees. [S]<list><item>Hydrocortisone 10 mg on waking, 5 mg at noon and 5 mg at 4 pm (20 mg a day).</item><item>Fludrocortisone 0.1 mg once daily.</item><item>Sodium, potassium and renin in six weeks.</item><item>Sick-day rules reviewed; he stated them back and gave a practice injection on the trainer.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Raised TSH with positive thyroid peroxidase antibodies (R94.6)</content>. TSH 7.4 mIU/L with free T4 1.0 ng/dL and thyroid peroxidase antibodies 286 IU/mL at intake: probable autoimmune thyroiditis. A newly raised TSH should be confirmed before treatment, and glucocorticoid replacement is already established. [S]<list><item>Repeat TSH and free T4 at the six-week visit, two months after the first test.</item><item>If the TSH is still raised, start levothyroxine.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic low back pain after lumbar disc surgery (M96.1)</content>. Oswestry 32% at intake, moderate disability. Bone density scan today: lumbar spine Z-score -1.3 and total hip Z-score -0.6, both within the expected range for his age despite earlier corticosteroid exposure. [S]<list><item>Continue physical therapy twice a week with a daily home program.</item><item>Swim three times a week.</item><item>Wear the brace for driving only, and leave it off at home.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Glucocorticoid dose: weight, energy, and signs of under- or over-replacement</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Afternoon tiredness on 30 mg a day, weight 66.8 kg, no signs of excess; reduced to 20 mg a day in three doses on the consultant&apos;s advice</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure lying and standing</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>110/70 lying, 96/62 standing: postural drop of 14 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Mineralocorticoid: salt craving, edema, sodium and potassium</td><td>After any fludrocortisone change, then at least yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Sodium 134 and potassium 5.2 mmol/L at intake, with salt craving; fludrocortisone raised to 0.1 mg today; recheck in six weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Plasma renin activity</td><td>After any fludrocortisone change, then at least yearly; aim for the upper normal range</td><td>Up to date</td><td>9.6 ng/mL/h at intake, raised; recheck in six weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sick-day rules, emergency injection kit, steroid card, and medical ID</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Rules stated back; practice injection on the trainer; kit, card and medical ID with him</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Cause: 21-hydroxylase antibodies</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Positive at intake (18 U/mL): autoimmune adrenalitis; no adrenal imaging needed</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH and free T4</td><td>Confirmatory repeat 2 to 3 months after a newly abnormal TSH, before treatment (CMID); TSH at least yearly in adrenal insufficiency</td><td>Due</td><td>TSH 7.4 mIU/L with normal free T4 and positive antibodies at intake; repeat at the six-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Other associated autoimmune disease: vitamin B12, A1c, and celiac serology</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly or with symptoms</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Vitamin B12 455 pg/mL, A1c 5.1%, tissue transglutaminase IgA 1.1 U/mL with total IgA 214 mg/dL at intake: no other associated disease found</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bone density (DXA)</td><td>Once now because of earlier corticosteroid exposure; repeat only if risk rises</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Lumbar spine Z-score -1.3, total hip Z-score -0.6: within the expected range for age</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Oswestry Disability Index</td><td>Each back pain review</td><td>Up to date</td><td>32% at intake; next at the six-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise therapy, physical therapy, and brace use</td><td>Weekly to twice weekly physical therapy, daily home program</td><td>Completed today</td><td>First session done; home exercises daily; brace for driving only</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Analgesic use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acetaminophen 1 g once or twice a day; no NSAID or opioid</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Endocrinology review</td><td>At diagnosis, then at least yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Desai today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No adrenal crisis and no emergency admission through nine months. In progress; target 1948-10-19 [S]</item><item>Hydrocortisone at the lowest dose that keeps him well, within 15 to 25 mg a day, with his weight back toward his usual 72 kg and no signs of over-replacement. In progress; target 1948-10-19 [S]</item><item>Sodium and potassium normal, renin in the upper normal range, and no postural drop in blood pressure by the six-week review. In progress; target 1948-03-09 [S]</item><item>He can state the sick-day rules and give the emergency injection by the two-week visit. Outcome by 1948-01-26: Stated the rules and gave a practice injection on a trainer [S]</item><item>Oswestry Disability Index below 20% by three months, with the brace used only for long travel. In progress; target 1948-04-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Diagnosis and Treatment of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2016); Guidelines for the treatment of hypothyroidism: prepared by the American Thyroid Association Task Force on Thyroid Hormone Replacement (2014); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); 2022 American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Prevention and Treatment of Glucocorticoid-Induced Osteoporosis (2022); Osteoporosis in men: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline (2012); American College of Gastroenterology Guidelines Update: Diagnosis and Management of Celiac Disease (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypothyroidism monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week visit with sodium, potassium, creatinine, renin, TSH, free T4 and the Oswestry score. Call the clinic for fever, vomiting or feeling faint, and follow the sick-day rules. Next scheduled visit 1948-03-09: six-week review after the dose changes: electrolytes, renin, repeat thyroid tests, and back pain. [S]</paragraph></text>

